#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:858
msgid "11. Sets"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:860
msgid "What is a set?"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:862
msgid "A **set** is an **unordered** collection of **unique** values - the sets of mathematics."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:865
msgid "No duplicates: each value is in a set **once**."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:866
msgid "No order: a set has no first and no last item, and no indexes."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:867
msgid "The items must be **immutable** (*hashable*): numbers, strings, tuples - not lists."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:868
msgid "The set itself is mutable: you can add and remove items."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:870
msgid "Sets are used to test membership, to remove duplicates, and to compare groups: union, intersection, difference (section 12)."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:873
msgid "Create a set"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:875
msgid "A set is written in **curly brackets**:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:890
msgid "`{}` is not an empty set"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:891
msgid "`{}` is an empty **dictionary** (session 6). An empty set is `set()`:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:898
msgid "Unique values: `SELECT DISTINCT`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:900
msgid "`set()` makes a set from any sequence - the duplicates disappear:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:913
msgid "On the data - the distinct products and regions:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:924
msgid "The order of a set is not kept"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:925
msgid "A set does not remember the order in which values came. Print the same set of strings in two runs of a program, and the order can be different. When the order matters - in a report, in a test - use `sorted(the_set)`. (To remove duplicates **and** keep the first order: `list(dict.fromkeys(items))`, session 6.)"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:931
msgid "Add and remove"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:947
msgid "`pop()` removes and returns some item (which one is not defined), `clear()` empties the set. `len`, `in`, `min`, `max`, `sum` and `for` work as with lists."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:950
msgid "Sets and dictionaries"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:952
msgid "Both are **unordered** collections; both are **mutable**; both use **curly brackets**."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:954
msgid "A dictionary is a collection of `key: value` **pairs**; a set is a collection of **values** only - like the keys of a dictionary without the values."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:957
msgid "Dictionaries are the topic of session 6."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:959
msgid "Sets are fast for `in`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:961
msgid "A set is a **hash table** inside: Python calculates from the value where it must be, and looks only there. A list is searched item by item, from the start. For `in` on a large collection, a set is much faster; for going through all items, and for access by index, a list is better."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:984
msgid "So when you check many values against a long list of allowed codes - a nomenclature - put the codes into a set first."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:987
msgid "Advanced: `frozenset`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/03-lists-tuples-sets.md:988
msgid "A `frozenset` is an immutable set. Because it cannot change, it can be an item of a set, or a key of a dictionary:"
msgstr ""

